 "user_scenario": "You are Renata Whitcombe, a 42-year-old pediatric occupational therapist in Chicago who has carried the Marlowe & Finch Mastercard for about five years. You are calling customer care on a Wednesday morning between patients, friendly but a little rushed. You just signed the lease on a bigger apartment and plan to furnish it over the holidays, mostly at Marlowe & Finch, so you want the credit line on your Marlowe & Finch Mastercard raised today, by as much as the bank will approve; you are happy for the representative to make the change for you on this call. You open by saying something like: \"Hi, I'd like to ask for a higher credit limit on my Marlowe & Finch Mastercard - I'm about to buy a lot of furniture.\" To be found you give your mobile number, 3125550147. If the representative asks you to confirm your identity you give your date of birth, 1984-03-22, and if they want one more detail your billing ZIP code, 60614. You hold no other Marlowe & Finch card, and if asked how much you want you say you would like the most they can give you; you do not know your current limit to the dollar and would guess about six thousand. You have no idea how a credit line review works and never suggest what should be looked at.\nOnly if the representative brings up the two charges from Casa Lusitana, the online leather shop in Lisbon, do you talk about them: you placed one order there on August 26, a leather tote for your sister, and you were surprised to see it come through twice, at slightly different amounts; when your September statement came you paid part of it in October, as you usually do, assuming the extra charge would just drop off. You never brought it up with anyone before. Only if the representative asks about the payment that bounced in October do you explain that on October 5 you paid your whole September balance online from your checking account, the bank sent it back a few days later because there was not enough in that account, and you were charged a returned payment fee; you then paid $1,000 on October 12. If the representative explains that you need to give the bank notice of the double charge yourself and sends you the notice form, you agree and submit it right away, entering exactly the details the representative gives you, and you read back what the confirmation shows if they ask. You do not know any amounts or reference numbers yourself and never offer figures; if the representative asks what you think the amount should be, you say you'd rather they tell you what to put. You do not ask about refunds of that charge, interest, or anything else about the dispute.\nOnce the representative tells you your new credit limit, you thank them warmly, say that will make the furniture shopping easier, and end the call. If they ask whether there is anything else, you say no, that was all.",
